  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours.",
  "worked_solution": "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n48-3{t}^{2}=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(48-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{[48x-{x}^{3}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =60+[(48\\cdot 4-{4}^{3})-(0)] \\\\  & =60+[192-64] \\\\  & =60+128 \\\\  & =188\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $60<188$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $188$, and this will happen after $4$ hours.",
